  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Chance for rain showers by later this week,\nthough little to no drought improvement anticipated.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA low pressure system centered over Hudson Bay will move\neastward across Quebec on Thursday, dragging a cold front\nthrough New England into the evening and overnight hours. Some\ndeterministic models indicate a weak low pressure develops along\nthis front in coastal Maine, though models diverge on the exact\nspeed and strength of this feature. Highest chance of\nprecipitation will be along the coast Thursday evening and night\nwhere most moisture will be located, up to 45-75% chance, while\ndry air and lack of strong forcing should win out in northern\nMaine with only 5-30% chance. Any showers will be light, with\nonly a trace to 0.15 inches anticipated, though drizzle and mist\nare also possible as a marine layer sets up along the coast. The\narea remains under D0-D2 (abnormally dry to severe drought)\nconditions, and the light rain showers or drizzle later this\nweek are not expected to improve this. However, the cold front\nshould result in cold air advection on Friday to usher in a\nperiod of cooler temperatures into the weekend as the low\npressure heads into the Canadian maritimes. Friday could also be\nbreezy across the forecast area with gusts 25-35 mph possible,\nand a few light showers are possible (10-35% chance) on Friday\nmorning across northern Maine as a trough moves through.",
